mise 100 billion dollars a year  in climate funding from rich countries  to go directly to adaptation measures  now only a quarter is currently  allocated to it and the most vulnerable  countries in the world say that will be  too little and it's rapidly becoming too  late so mikhail i don't know if you know  but we've lost a michael mann he dropped  out behind me there while i was  introducing you but hopefully we'll get  him back in a moment but first of all so  this call from these uh climate  vulnerable nations these island states  saying that cop 26 is make or break for  them  what do you think it's going to be is it  going to be make or break will the rich  nation step up and give them the help  they need  when we look in the eyes of small island  states leaders we are looking at the eye  of climate change and first of all  there's a moral imperative to make that  happen and i think cop 26 is an  excellent opportunity for world leaders  to do that and to uh understand that  this is it  now this is it because climate change is  not something that's happening in 2030  or in 2013 or in 2050 climate change is  already here and has already been here  for small island states as we've seen  in your excellent piece about granada  the climate finance that has been  promised as  as we know is completely insufficient to  meet the adaptation needs of those  small island states  and in a way it is almost like choosing  between a vaccine and a treatment if we  start weighing off adaptation versus  mitigation  climate change uh is already impacting  those countries in a way that is  irreversible for some of them  but i think that we have an interesting  opportunity here because small island  states have proved in the past  especially around the paris agreement  that they have significant power to  impact those debates well yes okay they  were the one they were the ones who  spearheaded for example the crucial  language on well below two degrees that  um managed to get into t