 presenting climate change as an  unsolvable problem um  something that will lead to our  extinction but you know it's hard to be  too optimistic with some of these  low-lying island nations they really are  facing an existential crisis they will  lose their homes they will lose  everything they have and so some of that  is locked in it's baked in there's a  certain amount of additional sea level  rise that's baked in if we can hold  warming to one and a half degrees  celsius which is we already saw earlier  we heard we need to bring carbon  emissions down by 50 within this decade  but if we can do that we can probably  hold warming below one and a half  degrees celsius probably limit sea level  rise to under a meter where there's  still the potential to adapt to those  impacts okay we're going to move on to  our second subject now because here the  environment agency's chief has given us  a stark warning saying we must adapt or  die when it comes to dealing with  extreme weather brought about by climate  change and similar language has been  used by world leaders and environmental  groups to shock people into action but  does it always work mikhail to you first  of all i'm just going to put a statistic  to you apparently 56 of young people  think that humanity is doomed so  should we talk in these kind of terms  does it frighten people and put people  off the climate change cause or does it  prompt people to take action  we need to do both  we need to make sure that everyone is  aware that we are in a place and in a  time that we need to act right now  and unfortunately being in the comfort  zone and telling us no you know what  everything is fine and you just have to  use some plastic straws and everything  will be fine that's not going to cut it  and we know that so we need to describe  reality as it is in reality as it is is  pretty scary but if we stop there  that's not going to take us anywhere so  the next thing that we have to do is be  angry about it a little bit and it's um  and we need to be angry because we did  this and we made decisions along the way  the collectively that led to this  but if we stop there that's not good  enough either because right the next  step that we need to take is agency  and everything in all the science and  all the ipcc reports and everything that  we know teaches us and tells us that we  still can make a difference  so we take this um sadness and we take  this anger and we channel it into agency  that's where we need to go so adapt or  die is exactly a call for action it is  um if we don't  then we will uh suffer great  circumstances but we can okay michael  what do you think a message of fear or  message of hope what works best  i think mccall you know uh spelled it  out right here um it's urgency and  agency yes we're already facing  devastating consequences of human-caused  climate change and it will get much  worse if we fail to take action that's  the urgency we can see the urgency we're  literally seeing the the you know the  the dangerous and damaging impacts of  climate change play out in real time now  that's the urgency the agency as mccall  says we have time to prevent the worst  it's going to be a monumental challenge  and cop26 is where we have to make it  happen this is our moment if we are  going to keep warming uh to a level that  we can still adapt to that will remain  within our adaptive capacity we need to  see substantial action now and that's  what cop 26 has to be about okay we will  leave it on that note with mikhail  nodding mikhail michael good to see you  thanks very much indeed
 

